#edb/ref 17592186192391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192686 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1039 "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192798 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1039 "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192800 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1045 "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192934 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1ee2078f-2d7b-5d91-844b-dd810c5980c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $5.1,\\,4.2,\\,6.3$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7dcf32e-5721-58b3-a00b-d1865bbf3e18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193200 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =5.1+4.2+6.3=15.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 15.6 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-15.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=3+(-15.6)=-12.6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193507 #edb/ref 1061 38135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e40004b-0de5-5409-b9f6-66d7010235cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193686 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193686 #edb/ref 1061 42290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193686 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd1280e1-8556-53f3-b24b-45e447786baf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193686 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193688 #edb/ref 1061 42304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d277bc8e-9565-50d6-8915-76af5cc26d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193689 #edb/ref 1061 42305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f38c06c-0588-54af-b61c-d056d9ae479e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193899 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed *continuous* random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x;\\theta)$ is the probability density function of $X_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots, n,$ and $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter.\n\nSimilar to the case of discrete random variables, the **log-likelihood function** is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's get some practice at calculating log-likelihood functions for some continuous random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193899 #edb/ref 1065 7005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193899 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c0170775-4a1b-50a6-8f90-446fcc21a159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193899 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 175921860454