186543420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543420 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "afd91053-3ad7-5610-9011-77c03294ee47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6.\n$$\n\n \nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543496 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(182.6-2\\theta \\cdot 41.2+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-91.3)+41.2\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-91.3)+41.2+(-5)=-55.1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543