ame{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.5+(-0.8)+(-1.3) \\\\  & =-1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}+(-0.8)^{2}+(-1.3)^{2} \\\\  & =2.58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(2.58-2\\theta \\cdot (-1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-1.29)+(-1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-1.29+(-1.6)+(-1.5)=-4.39.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $8,\\,3,\\,5,\\,10$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9473cd62-7434-5b05-96e0-e08528b86ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543530 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =8+3+5+10=26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 26 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-26)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+(-26)=-22.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543544 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $0.7,\\,0.6,\\,-0.5,\\,1.7,\\,-1.1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417